prompts.copy_content:
& {
Set-StrictMode -Version Latest
$ErrorActionPreference = "Stop"
$Repo = xyz_repo_or_path
$AgentSpec = xyz_gnhf_agent_spec
$AgentCommand = xyz_agent_command
$AgentProbeArgs = @(xyz_agent_probe_args)
$TimeoutSeconds = 20

if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath "$Repo\.git")) {
throw "Git repository not found: $Repo"
}

$Dirty = @(git -C $Repo status --porcelain)
if ($Dirty.Count -gt 0) {
$Dirty
throw "Repository must be clean before an unattended run."
}

$Gnhf = Get-Command gnhf -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
if (-not $Gnhf) { throw "gnhf is not available on PATH." }

$Resolved = Get-Command $AgentCommand -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
if (-not $Resolved) { throw "Agent command not found: $AgentCommand" }

if ($AgentSpec -notmatch "^acp:" -and $Resolved.Source -match "\.(cmd|bat)$") {
throw "Native GNHF agent resolves to a CMD/BAT shim. Prove an agentPathOverride or use a proven ACP/native executable before running overnight."
}

$Psi = [Diagnostics.ProcessStartInfo]::new()
$Psi.FileName = $Resolved.Source
$Psi.UseShellExecute = $false
$Psi.RedirectStandardOutput = $true
$Psi.RedirectStandardError = $true
$Psi.CreateNoWindow = $true
foreach ($Arg in $AgentProbeArgs) { [void]$Psi.ArgumentList.Add($Arg) }

$Process = [Diagnostics.Process]::new()
$Process.StartInfo = $Psi
[void]$Process.Start()
if (-not $Process.WaitForExit($TimeoutSeconds * 1000)) {
try { $Process.Kill($true) } catch {}
throw "Agent probe timed out after $TimeoutSeconds seconds."
}

$Stdout = $Process.StandardOutput.ReadToEnd().Trim()
$Stderr = $Process.StandardError.ReadToEnd().Trim()
if ($Process.ExitCode -ne 0) {
throw "Agent spawn probe failed with exit code $($Process.ExitCode): $Stderr"
}

[pscustomobject]@{
Repo = (Resolve-Path $Repo).Path
AgentSpec = $AgentSpec
AgentCommand = $AgentCommand
ResolvedPath = $Resolved.Source
ExitCode = $Process.ExitCode
Output = $Stdout
ReadyForP38 = $true
} | Format-List
}

INSTALL OR REPAIR EXECUTABLE EVALS FOR THE TARGET SKILL NOW. DO NOT RETURN ONLY A RUBRIC, TEST PLAN, EXAMPLE PROMPTS, OR PERFORMANCE ADVICE.

Repo: resolve the current repository from the environment.
Branch: preserve existing work and use an isolated branch or worktree.
Target skill: xyz_skill_id_or_path
Sprint: Skill Correctness, Weakness, and Efficiency Evaluation.
Owned scope: the target skill's eval cases, fixtures, runner, result schema, scoring contract, performance and token instrumentation, focused CI, and directly related skill, trigger, capability, or factoring repairs.
Forbidden scope: unrelated skills, production deployment, provider credentials, hidden benchmark data, destructive cleanup, force-push, validator weakening, metric gaming, and unbounded prompt rewriting.
Expected artifacts: tracked eval contract, executable runner, versioned cases and fixtures, machine-readable results, before/after correctness and efficiency evidence, focused CI, commit SHA, and push or PR evidence.

MISSION
Build an eval system that does more than confirm the skill can run. It must identify bugs, inefficiencies, routing mistakes, missing functionality, weak boundaries, excessive context, redundant tool use, and token-heavy implementation choices; guide repairs through reproducible feedback; validate correctness at unit and integration levels; and prove that efficiency improvements preserve or improve task quality.

1. INSPECT THE COMPLETE SKILL SURFACE
   - Read repository governance, the target skill, manifests, deterministic triggers, capabilities, routing, scripts, schemas, workflows, tests, known failures, proof vocabulary, generated-output policy, open PRs, and recent commits.
   - Identify every component the skill selects, invokes, reads, writes, or hands off to. Do not evaluate the skill file in isolation when the real behavior spans routing, capability, code, and workflow surfaces.

2. DEFINE THE EVAL CONTRACT BEFORE REPAIR
   For every case record:
   - case ID and version;
   - purpose and weakness class;
   - input and preconditions;
   - expected trigger and route;
   - expected capability calls, artifacts, response properties, and proof level;
   - forbidden outcomes;
   - deterministic assertion or explicit scored rubric;
   - latency, tool-call, context, and token budgets when measurable;
   - completion gate and proof ceiling.

3. BUILD A COMPLETE CASE MATRIX
   Include:
   - positive activation cases;
   - negative and near-miss routing cases;
   - boundary and overlap cases against neighboring skills;
   - malformed-input and missing-precondition cases;
   - forbidden-condition and safe-refusal cases;
   - historical regression cases from real defects;
   - unit tests for deterministic helpers, schemas, triggers, and capability contracts;
   - integration tests across skill + trigger + capability + workflow + artifact;
   - end-to-end or provider-backed cases only in a separately labeled lane with honest cost, authentication, and runtime blockers.

4. IDENTIFY WEAKNESSES, NOT JUST FAILURES
   The eval must detect and classify:
   - functional bugs and incorrect outputs;
   - missing functionality or unhandled conditions;
   - false-positive and false-negative skill selection;
   - ambiguous ownership or overlapping triggers;
   - brittle assumptions and unreproducible behavior;
   - repeated instructions, duplicated context, unnecessary files, redundant tool calls, and avoidable model turns;
   - excessive prompt, completion, or total token use;
   - latency, cost, memory, or artifact-size regressions;
   - proof inflation, unsafe fallback behavior, and hidden application logic in prose.

5. ESTABLISH A REPRODUCIBLE BASELINE
   Before optimization, capture the current:
   - pass/fail and scored-quality results;
   - routing precision and recall where applicable;
   - unit, integration, and regression coverage;
   - latency distribution;
   - tool and model call counts;
   - prompt, completion, cached, and total token usage when available;
   - context files/bytes loaded;
   - cost and failure/retry rate;
   - produced artifacts and proof ceiling.
   Do not claim improvement without a comparable baseline and stable fixture set.

6. USE FEEDBACK-DRIVEN REPAIR
   - Reproduce each valid weakness with a failing case before changing behavior when feasible.
   - Apply test-driven development: add or strengthen the failing eval, make the smallest correct repair, then rerun focused and regression suites.
   - Use profiling or execution traces for performance and token defects rather than guessing.
   - Keep a finding-to-repair ledger linking each defect to evidence, changed files, validating cases, and disposition.
   - Never weaken an assertion, threshold, fixture, or quality rubric merely to make the suite green.

7. OPTIMIZE WITH SOUND FACTORING PRINCIPLES
   Reduce token use and execution waste without hiding behavior or lowering quality:
   - keep each skill bounded to one coherent activation and outcome;
   - split mixed domains, unrelated triggers, or oversized context requirements;
   - merge only materially identical routes and procedures;
   - move deterministic work into code, schemas, registries, validators, and workflows;
   - load only the skill instructions and repository context required for the selected task;
   - replace repeated prose with canonical references or machine-readable contracts;
   - remove duplicate calls, unnecessary intermediate summaries, and repeated repository scans;
   - prefer reusable capabilities over re-explaining operations in every prompt;
   - preserve clarity, safety, correctness, and debuggability while reducing token and latency budgets.

8. PROVE OPTIMIZATION DID NOT DEGRADE QUALITY
   Compare baseline and candidate results on the same versioned cases. A performance or token change passes only when:
   - required correctness and safety gates remain green;
   - no critical regression is introduced;
   - routing quality is preserved or improved;
   - artifact and proof contracts remain satisfied;
   - token, latency, call-count, or cost improvement is measured rather than asserted.
   Report regressions and tradeoffs honestly; do not optimize a proxy while degrading the real task.

9. PRODUCE MACHINE-READABLE RESULTS
   The runner must emit case IDs, versions, target implementation SHA, pass/fail or score, rule and weakness IDs, evidence, timing, call counts, token metrics when available, baseline/candidate deltas, evaluator metadata, artifacts, and proof ceiling. Deterministic failures must return nonzero and block focused CI.

10. VALIDATE AND DELIVER
   - Run the complete focused eval matrix.
   - Run target unit and integration tests, repository validators, build/static checks, secret and generated-output hygiene, and git diff --check.
   - Repair valid failures within owned scope.
   - Commit the coherent eval and skill repair.
   - Push normally and open or update the intended PR when authorized.

FINAL REPORT
Repo, branch, target skill and related capability/trigger surfaces, weakness inventory, case matrix, runner and schema paths, unit/integration/regression results, baseline and candidate performance/token metrics, finding-to-repair ledger, changed files, commit SHA, push or PR state, proof achieved, proof ceiling, final Git status, and one exact next command that runs the eval and opens or prints its canonical result artifact.

BUILD A CLIENT-SCOPED PROMPT PACK AND LOCAL PROFILE IMPORT SYSTEM FOR THE PROMPT KIT. IMPLEMENT THE PRODUCT; DO NOT STOP AT A MOCKUP OR SCHEMA NOTE.

Repo: xyz_repo_or_path
Client/domain: xyz_client_or_domain
Example prompt collection, if supplied: xyz_prompt_pack_or_context

MISSION
Let a client or domain expert bring their own prompt library into the Prompt Kit without requiring those prompts to become part of the global canonical website. A security expert, for example, should be able to define several security prompt types, assign a stable color to each type, import many prompts at once, and then search, filter, favorite, navigate, export, and re-import them as naturally as built-in prompts.

GLOBAL VS LOCAL BOUNDARY
- Canonical/global prompts remain read-only unless a separate explicit registry-promotion workflow is requested.
- Client prompts live in a local profile overlay or cached profile owned by the user/device/account boundary already supported by the application.
- Imported prompts must not rewrite built-in IDs, built-in Favorites, global display order, generated-site source, or canonical registry files merely because a local pack was loaded.
- Use the repository's existing stable-origin storage/portability mechanism when it fits; do not invent a second browser-state system without evidence that the current one cannot support the contract.

VERSIONED PROMPT-PACK CONTRACT
Define a compact data-only JSON contract with at least:
- `schema_version` and `profile_id`;
- display name and optional description/source metadata;
- `types`: stable type ID, human label, associated color, and optional display order;
- `prompts`: stable local prompt ID, name, type ID, copy content, keywords/tags, and only the metadata actually needed by Prompt Kit behavior;
- explicit merge/replace semantics and a normalized export form.
Keep user-state such as Favorites separate from the authored pack unless the current portability contract explicitly owns it. Namespace imported identities, for example `local:<profile_id>:<prompt_id>`, so collisions with canonical `P##` records or another client's pack cannot silently overwrite data.

SLEEK, STABLE IMPORT INTERFACE
Reuse current visual and interaction patterns. Provide one clear local-profile surface with:
1. JSON file upload and drag/drop when the existing app supports it;
2. a paste-JSON/text area for copy/paste workflows;
3. immediate schema validation with precise field-level errors;
4. a preview showing profile name, prompt count, type groups, type/color chips, duplicate/conflict counts, and normalized IDs;
5. explicit Import, Merge, Replace, Cancel, Export, and Remove/Clear actions only where their semantics are safe and deterministic;
6. keyboard access, responsive mobile behavior, readable focus states, and no modal trap or overlapping controls.
Do not make a user edit raw internal IDs just to import a normal pack.

IMPORT SEMANTICS
- Parse input as data, never executable code.
- Normalize and validate before any persistent write.
- Reject unknown required-shape violations, duplicate local IDs, unresolved type references, unsupported colors, excessive size, or schema versions with no migration path.
- Define deterministic duplicate behavior for importing the same pack twice and for merging a newer version.
- Store the normalized local profile only after the preview passes validation and the user commits the import.
- Surface imported prompts through the same search index, type/color filters, dense navigation, detail view, copy action, and Favorites machinery as canonical prompts.
- Preserve Favorites across reloads and profile export/import according to the repository's current Favorites ownership contract; never erase unrelated saved prompts during a profile import.

SECURITY AND PRIVACY
Treat uploaded/pasted JSON as hostile input. No `eval`, dynamic script execution, HTML injection, unsafe URL execution, prototype pollution, or direct DOM insertion from unescaped prompt fields. Enforce practical file/record/string limits. Do not send local prompt packs to a server or third party unless the product explicitly owns that sync behavior and the user has opted into it. Do not persist credentials, secrets, or hidden private metadata merely because they appear in a prompt pack.

VERSIONING AND PORTABILITY
Make export deterministic enough that an imported normalized pack can be exported and re-imported without semantic drift. Define schema-version rejection/migration behavior. Preserve type colors and grouping. Support moving the profile between compatible Prompt Kit installations without requiring a global-site rebuild.

PROTOTYPE BEFORE POLISH
Build the thinnest real end-to-end slice first: one sample security profile with multiple type/color groups and enough prompts to exercise filtering and Favorites. Use that working slice to expose schema, UX, storage, and collision problems before polishing. Do not substitute a fake UI or hard-coded fixture for the real import path.

VALIDATE
At minimum prove:
- file upload import and pasted import produce the same normalized profile;
- malformed/hostile JSON is rejected without persistent mutation;
- canonical prompts are unchanged;
- local namespacing prevents collisions;
- three or more type/color groups render and filter correctly;
- imported prompts can be copied and favorited like native prompts;
- Favorites and local profiles survive the supported reload/stable-origin lifecycle;
- export -> clear -> re-import preserves normalized pack identity and behavior;
- merge/replace and version migration paths are deterministic;
- generated canonical Prompt Kit parity and existing portability/search/filter tests remain green.

DELIVER
Implement the smallest durable schema/runtime/interface/test changes in existing owners. Report the pack contract, UI entry point, storage owner, collision/versioning rules, security limits, prototype iterations, focused validation, generated-site parity, commit/PR/merge state, and the exact artifact or URL that exercises the finished local-profile flow.

DESIGN THE PROGRAM BEFORE BROAD IMPLEMENTATION. PROTOTYPE THE ASSOCIATED CALL STACKS SO THE ARCHITECTURE IS TESTED AS EXECUTABLE SEAMS, NOT JUST BOXES AND ARROWS.

Repo/product: xyz_repo_or_product
Program/subsystem: xyz_program_scope
Primary user outcomes: resolve from current conversation/spec/repository evidence

MISSION
Bridge the gap between repository doctrine/harness and feature implementation. Define how the program should actually be decomposed, where state and decisions live, how calls move from entrypoint to domain behavior to side effects, and how failures return. Then build thin executable call-stack prototypes for the most consequential journeys so the proposed design earns confidence before broad product code is committed.

1. RECOVER THE PROGRAM, NOT JUST THE FILE TREE
Start from user outcomes, domain language, invariants, actors, inputs/outputs, lifecycle, failure cases, and explicit constraints already present in the conversation/spec/repository. Distinguish:
- GOVERNANCE: rules for how work is performed;
- HARNESS: maps, validators, workflows, skills, artifact/proof infrastructure;
- PROGRAM DESIGN: runtime/application modules, interfaces, state ownership, orchestration, adapters, and call flow;
- IMPLEMENTATION: the full production behavior built against that design.
Do not use a harness document as a substitute for program architecture. Do not invent requirements merely to fill a diagram.

1A. WIDEN THE DESIGN SPACE BEFORE CONVERGING
When uncertainty is material, inspect relevant external repositories or structurally analogous systems using source/tests/config. Synthesize 2-4 materially different candidates and compare the same acceptance criteria. Creativity is disciplined recombination, not novelty. Use P97 Open-Source Prior-Art & Gap Analyst for broad ecosystem research; verify license/security/dependency implications before direct reuse.

1B. ACQUIRE COHESIVE REPOSITORY CONTEXT — NOT ONE ISOLATED FILE
Inspect the relevant tree, root manifests/configuration, entrypoints, routing/composition, state owners, dependencies, tests, and persistence/external boundaries together. If direct access is unavailable, use bounded Repomix/code2prompt or tree-plus-root-config context excluding secrets/vendor/generated bulk. Do not treat a single pasted file as sufficient evidence; narrow claims to available context.

1C. CHOOSE THE DEPLOYMENT OPERATING MODEL FROM EVIDENCE
Compare managed PaaS/serverless; one host with OCI containers via Docker or Podman; Kubernetes/managed orchestration only for proved need. “millions someday” is not proof. Aggregate database/storage size is not application-state demand: split footprint by owner: durable application/business state vs telemetry/log/diagnostic exhaust; inspect retention/TTL/rotation and write rate/cardinality. A small application database buried under a telemetry accident is not tier proof; contain it; rerun against the irreducible app-state/workload floor. Track p95/p99/SLO, queues/RTO/RPO/toil/cost. Keep this branch progressive-disclosure: do not preload tier-specific Docker/Podman/Kubernetes detail; inspect it only after current evidence makes that tier decision-relevant.

2. BUILD A PRECISE DOMAIN VOCABULARY
Name concepts by what they own and decide. Identify:
- domain entities/value objects or equivalent core records;
- commands/queries/events where useful;
- state machines/lifecycle states;
- policies/invariants;
- services/orchestrators;
- ports/interfaces;
- adapters to external systems;
- persistence/cache/message/network/UI/CLI boundaries.
Prefer stable domain vocabulary over generic `Manager`, `Utils`, `Helper`, or architecture-fashion names that hide responsibility.

3. DESIGN DEEP MODULES AND CLEAN SEAMS
For each proposed module record:
- responsibility and owned state/data;
- public interface;
- hidden implementation complexity;
- callers and dependencies;
- side effects;
- failure/error contract;
- observability;
- test seam.
Favor modules that provide meaningful leverage: a small, understandable interface controlling a larger coherent behavior. Keep related logic local. Introduce adapters where an external dependency or volatile implementation deserves a seam, not merely to add layers. Testability should fall naturally out of the interface rather than require invasive mocking of internals.

4. MAKE OWNERSHIP AND DEPENDENCY DIRECTION EXPLICIT
Every important mutable state, durable record, configuration decision, transaction boundary, retry policy, cache/invalidation rule, and external side effect needs one canonical owner. Draw or list dependency direction. Prevent cycles and split-brain ownership. Make clear which layer may call which and where translation occurs.

5. PROTOTYPE REPRESENTATIVE CALL STACKS
Choose the highest-risk/highest-value user journeys before broad implementation. For each, write the intended stack in execution order, for example:
USER/CLI/API EVENT
  -> ENTRYPOINT/CONTROLLER
  -> APPLICATION ORCHESTRATOR
  -> DOMAIN MODULE / STATE OWNER
  -> PORT / ADAPTER
  -> PERSISTENCE OR EXTERNAL SERVICE
  -> DOMAIN RESULT / ERROR
  -> PRESENTATION / RESPONSE
Then BUILD the thinnest executable vertical slice that traverses this stack. Use real domain decisions and real interfaces. Stub/fake only the true external boundary when live integration is unnecessary or unavailable. Do not fake the seam being evaluated.

For each prototype capture:
- input and starting state;
- functions/methods/modules actually traversed;
- data transformations;
- state mutation/transaction boundary;
- side effects;
- returned result;
- logs/traces useful for proof.

5A. END THE JOURNEY AT USER VALUE, NOT AN INTERMEDIATE SCREEN
Before accepting a user-facing call stack, name the terminal user value and classify each UI state as ENTRYPOINT, REQUIRED INTERMEDIATE, OPTIONAL INSPECTION, or TERMINAL ACTION. Opening a detail panel, focusing a close button, or navigating to an object is not success when the initiating command unambiguously means copy, execute, submit, export, or another deeper action. Prototype the stack through the terminal result and normal completion feedback. Remove redundant intermediate transitions when they add neither safety, comprehension, nor a genuinely separate inspection choice.

When the requested product includes preference-driven personalization, distinguish operational logs from product-usage events. Give semantic completion telemetry one owner, count the completed domain action rather than incidental views/focus, define its storage/privacy/reset boundary, and keep dashboards/recommendations as projections of that owner rather than second sources of truth.

6. PROTOTYPE FAILURE CALL STACKS TOO
A successful call stack alone is weak design proof. Exercise at least one consequential failure path per high-risk journey:
- validation/domain rejection;
- dependency timeout/unavailability;
- persistence conflict/partial failure;
- retry/idempotency path;
- authorization/privilege failure;
- stale/upgrade state when relevant.
Trace where the failure is classified, transformed, retried, compensated, surfaced, or logged. Do not let errors disappear into generic catch-all layers.

7. COMPARE SEAMS WHEN THE DESIGN IS UNCERTAIN
When two materially different module/interface boundaries are plausible, build bounded prototypes for both instead of debating abstractions indefinitely. Compare with the same criteria:
- correctness and invariant ownership;
- interface size/clarity;
- coupling and caller impact;
- testability;
- failure handling;
- state locality;
- change leverage;
- performance/operational complexity where relevant.
Keep one canonical design after evidence selects it; retire experimental alternatives rather than shipping parallel architectures without cause.

8. PROTOTYPE THE STATE MODEL
For stateful programs, model significant states and transitions before implementation spreads across callers. Exercise transitions through the same program interfaces used by the call-stack prototypes. Make illegal states/transitions explicit. Decide where persistence serialization ends and domain state begins.

9. DESIGN FOR TESTABILITY AND OBSERVABILITY
Test interfaces/seams; label unit/integration/live proof. Bound non-sensitive logs/traces/metrics; one owner controls volume/retention/cardinality. Diagnostics are not durable product state.

10. SECOND-PASS ARCHITECTURE CRITIQUE
After prototypes run, revisit the design:
- Which interface leaked implementation detail?
- Which module knows too much?
- Is important state owned twice or nowhere?
- Did a supposedly clean seam require pervasive mocks or caller knowledge?
- Is an adapter useful, or ceremony?
- Did the failure stack reveal missing error ownership?
- Would one likely future change touch too many unrelated modules?
Convert concrete findings into one more bounded prototype/refactor pass. Stop at a fixed point rather than polishing diagrams endlessly.

11. BOUNDARY WITH BUILD WORK
This prompt may create design artifacts, thin prototypes, interfaces, traces, and focused tests necessary to prove the design. It should not silently become a full product implementation sprint. Once the program design and representative call stacks are stable enough, hand broad implementation to the normal build executor while preserving the proved seams and tests. If missing harness infrastructure prevents reliable prototyping, route that specific gap to the harness owner rather than conflating it with program design.

DELIVER
Report: user outcomes/invariants; domain vocabulary; program module/interface map; state/data owners; dependency direction; success and failure call stacks; executable prototypes created; alternatives compared; tests/traces; design changes made after prototype evidence; unresolved decisions; proof ceiling; exact implementation seam ready for the next build sprint.

ANALYZE OPEN-SOURCE REPOSITORIES THAT HAVE ALREADY DONE THINGS LIKE THIS SO THAT WE CAN EMULATE THAT. DO NOT START BY INVENTING A LOCAL ARCHITECTURE IN A VACUUM.

Current repo/product: xyz_repo_or_product
Capability or problem: xyz_capability_or_problem
Known constraints: xyz_constraints_or_resolve_from_context

MISSION
Before deciding what this project should build, establish what credible open-source projects have already solved in the same problem space. Inspect real implementations deeply enough to separate reusable mechanics from slogans, then compare those mechanics against the current repository. The result must make two boundaries obvious: WHAT IS ALREADY AVAILABLE IN THE REAL WORLD and WHAT PROJECT-SPECIFIC GAP IS STILL WORTH DEVELOPING.

THIS PROMPT IS REFERENCE-ARCHITECTURE RESEARCH, NOT PORTFOLIO RANKING
Do not use stars, popularity, or a polished README as a substitute for implementation evidence. Do not primarily rank which of our internal repositories should move first; that is a portfolio-priority job. Do not replace the repository's internal intent routing or implementation owner. This prompt supplies external precedent and a gap decision that downstream design/build prompts can consume.

1. LOCK THE LOCAL QUESTION FIRST
Recover the requested user outcome, current repository floor, existing implementation, non-negotiable constraints, and proof boundary. State the capability slice being researched in plain language. Search the current repo before the wider ecosystem so an existing local implementation is not misclassified as a gap.

2. FIND A BOUNDED REFERENCE SET
Search for genuinely relevant open-source repositories that implement the same capability, a close architectural analogue, or a proven subsystem that could inform it. Prefer a small diverse reference set over a giant catalog. Include mature and simpler implementations when that contrast teaches something useful. For each selected reference record repository URL, project purpose, relevant branch/tag/commit or release identity when available, evidence date, license, and why it belongs in the set.

Do not select a project merely because it uses the same buzzword. Reject references whose user problem, scale assumptions, runtime, language constraints, security model, deployment model, or maintenance posture make the comparison misleading.

3. VERIFY IMPLEMENTATION, NOT MARKETING
Inspect the surfaces that prove how the relevant behavior actually works: source entrypoints, modules/services, state or data flow, configuration, schemas/contracts, tests, fixtures, extension points, failure handling, CI/validation, deployment/runtime assumptions, release history, and issue/discussion evidence when useful. A README can orient the search but cannot by itself prove an implementation claim.

Classify every material claim as one of:
- OBSERVED_IMPLEMENTED — directly supported by source/tests/config/runtime artifacts;
- DOCUMENTED_UNVERIFIED — stated by project docs but not confirmed in the inspected implementation;
- INFERRED — a reasonable architectural inference that must remain labeled;
- ABSENT — searched for and not found in the inspected scope.

Pin citations or repository paths closely enough that another agent can reproduce the observation. If the reference moved materially, refresh the evidence instead of carrying stale architecture forward.

4. EXTRACT MECHANISMS, NOT WHOLE PROJECTS
For each reference, extract only the capability slice that matters:
- user problem and entrypoint;
- architectural owner and boundary;
- state/data/control flow;
- extension or plugin seam;
- failure and recovery behavior;
- validation/eval strategy;
- operator/developer usability pattern;
- important performance/security/deployment assumptions;
- dependency and license constraints that affect adoption.

Describe the mechanism independently from its filenames so it can be compared across repositories. Preserve exact paths or symbols as evidence, but do not mistake one project's directory layout for a universal architecture.

5. DISPOSITION EVERY REFERENCE PATTERN
Use a compact pattern ledger with one of these dispositions:
- ADOPT — the mechanism fits our constraints closely and can be reproduced through our existing owners;
- ADAPT — the principle is useful but our contracts, scale, runtime, UX, language, security, or deployment assumptions require a different implementation;
- REJECT — evidence shows the pattern would create the wrong tradeoff or violate a local constraint;
- UNKNOWN — evidence is insufficient; name the exact missing proof.

EMULATE MECHANISMS, NOT CODE BLINDLY. Reuse interfaces, workflows, validation strategies, state models, failure policies, or interaction patterns when they fit. Do not copy source merely because it exists. If direct code reuse is contemplated, verify license compatibility, provenance, dependency cost, security implications, and maintenance ownership first.

6. BUILD THE SOLVED-BASELINE VS PRIORITIZED-GAP MAP
Compare the external reference mechanisms against the fresh current repository. Disposition each meaningful capability slice as:
- ALREADY_SOLVED_INTERNALLY — our repo already has an adequate canonical owner;
- AVAILABLE_TO_EMULATE_EXTERNALLY — a proven external mechanism exists and the local repo lacks or weakly implements it;
- PROJECT_SPECIFIC_GAP — the remaining need is genuinely local or no reference satisfies our constraints;
- EVIDENCE_GAP — the answer is still uncertain because implementation or runtime evidence is missing.

Make the delta explicit. Do not describe the external projects for pages and leave the reader to infer what we should build. The reference analysis exists to narrow the development target.

7. PRIORITIZE THE GAP THAT EARNS DEVELOPMENT
From AVAILABLE_TO_EMULATE_EXTERNALLY and PROJECT_SPECIFIC_GAP items, select the smallest high-leverage next target using current evidence: user value, architectural leverage, fit with existing owners, implementation tractability, dependency/collision risk, reversibility, and attainable proof. Prefer closing one consequential seam over copying an entire foreign architecture.

For the selected target state:
- exact local owner or seam;
- reference mechanism(s) worth emulating;
- local adaptations required;
- explicit non-goals and rejected patterns;
- expected artifact or behavior;
- focused validator/prototype/eval that will prove it;
- evidence that would invalidate the choice.

8. ADVANCE, DON'T END WITH A RESEARCH ESSAY
If the current task authorizes implementation and the environment can safely perform it, hand the selected gap directly into the repository's canonical design/build lane and continue from the fresh evidence floor. If implementation is not authorized or a user-only/runtime gate remains, produce one executable handoff with the exact owner, dependency, first action, expected artifact, and completion proof. Do not end with generic advice such as 'consider using project X.'

DELIVER
Keep the final result compact and decision-oriented:
- capability researched;
- reference repositories and evidence identities;
- reference matrix;
- ADOPT / ADAPT / REJECT / UNKNOWN pattern ledger;
- ALREADY_SOLVED_INTERNALLY / AVAILABLE_TO_EMULATE_EXTERNALLY / PROJECT_SPECIFIC_GAP / EVIDENCE_GAP map;
- prioritized development gap;
- exact local owner and next executable action;
- proof ceiling and unresolved evidence.

The success condition is not that we found impressive repositories. It is that real-world precedent made the local development gap clearer, smaller, and more defensible.
